result.overall:
Some of the draft's commitments are sound: rules fixed before outcomes, every result reported whatever it shows, one capped repair round, repairs behind default-off switches, and no acceptance on overall score. But its decision rules make "design limit" the default for any loss that fails thresholds chosen after the loss magnitudes were read. Several boundaries cannot pass those thresholds by construction:
- reading: oracle Q can recover at most 7.3 rows per run;
- state maintenance: no oracle covers it;
- ruled-out rows;
- A2-only causes;
- ES2009.

Two passages relabel unrepaired implementation defects as design: line 165 ("measured design limit") and line 168 ("the remaining losses are not implementation"). This contradicts the draft's own principle (line 19) and plan 23 (lines 20 and 358). It is the same move as the September "the cause is inherent" conclusion (state.md:186), which the bug audit later overturned.

The class boundary is left to the coder:
- the model, prompts, parameters (top_k=8, prune 12, reuse 0.80) and the harness reviewer have no stated status;
- D10 and D11 were written into Ch. 4 on 19 October, after cycle-1 results;
- the calibration examples pre-classify the largest observed loss categories.

The deciding coder is the author: unblinded to the oracle results and the model proposals, and aware of what each code triggers. Sub-cause granularity and the "at least half" rule are steering levers. The coded runs (A0 r1, A1 r2, A2 r1) are each the highest-filing-loss run of their arm. Only losses are coded, so evaluation-defect fixes move in one direction. The oracle inputs (script, model adjudications) are untracked and not hash-pinned. Step 3 needs ES2009 A1 runs that exist only after Step 5. The repair acceptance test is gold-referenced and sits inside run-to-run noise.

The fixes, in brief:
- separate classification from repair, so that unrepaired implementation-coded losses stay labelled as such and their mechanisms get *can't tell*;
- freeze a dated design-decision register with model and temperature as held-constant controls;
- pre-list the candidate causes, with deterministic diagnostics where they exist;
- double-code every decisive case, with disagreements resolved toward *can't tell*;
- code a random case sample across all runs, plus passes and RAG failures;
- pin the inputs by hash;
- accept conformance repairs on conformance alone, and implementation-choice repairs as one shot, fixed in advance and measured paired in the harness;
- add a "what was already read" section, with explicit ES2009 and end-of-Phase-C handling.

findings.problem:
Line 19 requires every remaining loss to be either repaired or shown to be a property of the design, the conversation or the evaluation. Step 4 then turns the repair thresholds into a labelling rule. A cause coded as an implementation defect is not repaired if it:
- falls below 10 rows;
- is under half of its boundary's recovered rows;
- exceeds the one-per-boundary cap; or
- is not approved.

Such a cause is then 'recorded as a measured design limit, not a to-do' (line 165). Line 168 adds 'If no cause qualifies... the remaining losses are not implementation'. This shifts the burden of proof: design becomes the default and implementation has to clear a bar. That contradicts the amendment's own principle, plan 23's 'Results limited by a defect show nothing ... in either direction' (P23:20), and D3's 'A failure coded as an implementation defect makes its mechanism can't tell' (P23:358).

