findings.proposed_change:
Replace condition 2 with a per-cause eligibility rule that applies to every boundary, kind and arm: "An implementation-coded primary cause is eligible when it accounts for at least N distinct gold values across the coded runs (N fixed here, e.g. 2, with the reason: a single value is an anecdote)."

State the measure that counts for each kind of cause:
- routing, including overwrites by non-gold values: net R recovery;
- extraction: E+R minus R;
- reading: Q;
- state rules: the rows E+R fails to deliver;
- ruled-out rows: their own oracle counts;
- A2-only causes: A2 runs.

Use the counts only to order repairs within the round's budget, never to label a cause. If an absolute threshold is kept, express it as a share of the rows that can reach that step, and justify it in the text.

findings.problem:
Clause 2 of 'implementation defect' reads 'an implementation choice the design does not require, for which an alternative within the same design exists'. It is met by:
- the generator model: Ch. 4 never names it; only the reproducibility appendix states gpt-5.4-mini for both systems;
- any prompt wording;
- every parameter value (embedding_top_k=8, resolver_prune_threshold=12, expansion_reuse_threshold=0.80);
- the autonomous arm's harness reviewer (_reviewer_would_accept).

Read literally, nearly all extraction losses (about 45 rows per run) are implementation defects: 'use a stronger model'. Read the other way, nothing is.

Clause 1 ('the code does not do what the documented design says') is close to vacuous. D10 and D11 were added to Ch. 4 on 19 October (5de9911; absent at c6fbdc0 and 94c049d), after the cycle-1 results, and D10 already says fragmentation and mis-filing 'surface in the evaluation'. The documented design was written to fit the observed losses.

The calibration examples pre-classify the largest observed loss categories:
- key assignment against a self-organised registry and one scalar value per key as architectural; these cover routing and overwrites, about a third of rows;
- the autonomous arm leaving conflicts open as a trade-off; this is A2's 36.7 rows per run, although 'who resolves conflicts' is an open decision that Step 0 itself adds.

The same codebook becomes plan 23's registered attribution coding. In Phase D an implementation code turns a mechanism's 'doesn't work' into 'can't tell', so the loophole also cuts the other way.

