findings.problem:
The amendment lists the deterministic services and says the recorded claims can be fed into 'the real services' with LLM_PROVIDER=none. That does not reproduce a run. Per meeting the runner does: ingest, gatekeeper, evidence, /extraction/run, /conflicts/evaluate, _internal_expand step 1 (an LLM key resolution per unkeyed or undefined-key claim; on a miss it parks the claim and drafts a key with the LLM, then embedding-based semantic dedup), step 2 (quality-gate acceptance, registry upsert, release of parked claims with re-evaluation), the A1 settle, the structural confirm, then the questions. With LLM_PROVIDER=none the resolver and generator fall back to rules, and semantic reuse silently degrades to exact reuse, so the keys and the registry would differ from the run. The harness therefore has to replay, from records, which key each claim got in each meeting, which claims were parked and released on which proposal and in what order, the key definitions, and the registry timeline. Only the ConflictService, resolution, proposal, settle, confirm and projection code then runs for real. The dump does not hold most of these inputs:
- claims carry only their final state_key, not the hint or the key history;
- there is no source_time, source_authority, created_at or span index;
- expansion proposals have no triggered_by_claim_ids, and there are no ParkedClaim rows;
- the coldstart report drops accepted_proposals.
They can be reconstructed from the trace:
- extraction outputs carry state_key_hint;
- every evaluation is logged as conflict_detection with claim_id and key;
- release evaluations sit just before each registry_expansion_review log, which names the proposal and its final key;
- the coldstart `reviewed` records give the meeting of acceptance, and the artifacts give authority and time.
None of this is said, and no build effort is given.

There are five problems with the E+R definition.

(a) It injects only for rows 'not carried by k'. Rows lost at the polarity or modality steps (extraction boundary) keep their wrong carriers and get no injection. On A0/A1 that is 12.2 of the 45.2 extraction-lost current rows per run (27%).

(b) 'Asserted' is wrong for candidate rows. current_unconfirmed arises only from non-asserted claims, so an asserted injection makes the row fail at the status step.

(c) 'The span's speaker and time' are not inputs the engine has:
- authority is the artifact's authority_level, 30 for every EL2020 meeting;
- time is the meeting's source time (created_at_source under A1, ingestion time under A0);
- the speaker is never used, and asserted_by is not set by LLM extraction;
- the A1 repairs also use said order (the span index) and created_at.

(d) Extraction confidence is decisive and unspecified. With no current fact, rivals equal on (authority, source time, confidence) with different values become a conflict, and same-meeting rivals are ranked by confidence.

(e) Two cases are undefined:
- 60 of 159 rows cite 2–5 lines, possibly in different spans;
- for 4 rows per run the cited span was never admitted, so it has no EvidenceSpan.

A carrier is decided per row (value × checkpoint). The Judge's sibling rule uses the other gold rows at k, so a claim can carry a value at one checkpoint and not at another. R needs one carrier set per run.

The probe on the six A0/A1 runs found:
- 1–3 claims per run carry values of two gold keys;
- 3–12 carry the value only through subject plus predicate, so the stored object (e.g. 'done now') does not carry it by itself;
- 0–1 gold keys per run would get a top-confidence tie between different wordings in one meeting, which becomes a conflict.

The definition also does not say:
- when a carrier is filed (at extraction, or at its recorded evaluation points);
- how gold keys are registered (scalar, open vocabulary, and when). Under A1, bug-5 refuses claims under undefined keys.

The registry timeline must stay as recorded even when the moved carrier was the claim that triggered a self-organised key. Otherwise other claims routed to that key hit an undefined key.

'Values that share a gold key share it' is right for EL2020: its only shared keys are supersession pairs (F25→F58, F36→F59), and the trace reads F25 and F36 as demoted rows that pass when superseded. It would be wrong for ES2009, where product.feature.decided holds 25 values and scalar sharing would make them supersede each other.

B3 adds questions that the old runs never answered:
- noise items with forbidden content;
- status items that can fail;
- unknown items about discussed-but-unsettled topics;
- more candidate/decided values.
A judge-only rescore (about $0.47–0.5 per EL2020 run) can only re-judge recorded answers against changed gold strings. New or reworded items need the reader re-run over the dump: about $1.12–1.38 per EL2020 run state-first (batch R). The answer replay would also use its final-status checkpoint approximation unless the snapshot option (from Q) is built.

The ES2009 adapter's `answered` step reads the sealed GPT-5.4 rescores. Those were scored on the old gold, while the adapter's gold rows come from the new gold (P1-8 affects about 12 questions; the three corrections bound 7). Rescoring them is a prerequisite that is not listed or costed.

